{
  "$comment": "Shell-neutral tool manifest read by both bootstraps (bootstrap.ps1 on Windows, bootstrap.sh on Linux). Each tool appears once. 'layer' is core (all roles) or dev (devbox only). 'packages' maps a package manager to its package id; a manager that is absent has no package for that tool on that manager. 'installer' names a non-package-manager install route handled by the bootstrap itself (uv: Astral's official installer, only when uv is not on PATH). Managers: winget, apt. 'release' (Linux only, handled by bootstrap.sh) pins a GitHub or vendor release binary for a tool with no apt package, installed into ~/.local/bin: 'version', a 'url' template with {version} and {arch}, a 'sha256' per arch (x64, arm64) that the download must match (taken from the upstream checksums file), and optional 'arch' (maps x64/arm64 to the name in the URL, default identity), 'archive' (tar.gz default, or zip), 'member' (path of the binary inside the archive, default the tool name; {version} and {arch} allowed) and 'versionArgs' (arguments that make the binary print its version, default 'version'). 'uvTools' are installed with `uv tool install` and pinned either by 'version' (a PyPI release, installed as name==version) or by 'source' (a git URL pinned to a full commit SHA, installed as `name @ source`; server-motd is not on PyPI). Bootstrap reinstalls when `uv tool list --show-version-specifiers` does not show that pin. Every core tool has a route on both platforms, so the profile's run-bootstrap hint clears after bootstrap. Supported Linux: Debian 13+ and Ubuntu 24.04+ (the apt ids below, notably eza and hexyl, need those releases). Dev tools: helm, terraform and tofu install from pinned releases on Linux. 'code' (VS Code) has no headless Linux route (no apt package; Microsoft's repo needs interactive key setup and a GUI session), so it is devbox-manual on Linux: install it by hand, and the dev layer skips it when absent.",
